00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.760
Let's go next to Ernest from Missouri. Ernest,&nbsp;
welcome to the program. What's on your mind today?&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:12.800
Speaker 2: Hi, David. I wanted to kind of preface&nbsp;
my question by saying I love the way you engage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:21.600
with all whose you don't necessarily agree with.&nbsp;
That's a skill I think, that's lost in the modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.560
political debate, or at least is slipping&nbsp;
away from us. Well, thank you for something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.200
Oh, you're welcome. What are your what are some&nbsp;
things you think we can do to, like, encourage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:40.880
people to engage in more civil debates and kind&nbsp;
of to broaden that out a get away from like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:47.200
talking points and more on the issues at hand.
Speaker 1: You know, my view on this shifts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:53.840
over time. One of the things I've been trying&nbsp;
recently is kind of putting it out there and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:59.200
people who have seen my appearances on some&nbsp;
places recently, like the Michael Knowles Show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.800
in Trigonometry might have noticed that I did,&nbsp;
as I say, up front, hey, do are we on the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.920
page that we want to have a good faith debate?&nbsp;
And we're not trying to do gotchas, but we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:16.320
trying to really solve problems or find shared&nbsp;
understanding, even if the other person doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.480
have any plan to do that. Usually they'll at least&nbsp;
say that that's their goal. And then you can use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:27.520
that as something to point back to and say, hey,&nbsp;
listen, let's let's skip the talking points. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.800
both said we wanted a good faith conversation and&nbsp;
we want to try to find some answers. Can't we just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:40.560
agree that X and it's sort of a cheap rhetorical&nbsp;
tool, but it might actually predispose people to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:45.040
maybe come in with a slightly better attitude. You&nbsp;
know, if you asked me a year ago, I might have had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:50.320
some different technique that I was trying&nbsp;
at the time. Another tool that can be useful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.560
is starting with where we agree. So, you know,&nbsp;
you could have a very contentious issue like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.520
I don't know, abortion or something like&nbsp;
that. But you could first start with, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:08.960
what what do we agree on? We we agree that murder&nbsp;
is bad. Certainly on all the instances where we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:19.440
agree as to what murder is, we agree that we want&nbsp;
people's ability to determine the best course for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.520
their life is something that should be priorities&nbsp;
that, you know, you lay out all of these areas of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.320
agreement and this can often kind of really narrow&nbsp;
the scope of the disagreement in a way that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.280
seems more manageable. So that's another idea. But&nbsp;
this is a really big topic. And if you ask me in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:38.480
six months, I might have a different perspective.
Speaker 2: Right. And so if you don't mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:43.120
I kind of had a follow up. Yeah, please. Which&nbsp;
is what I think is the more relevant thing. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:48.080
kind of wanted to frame that because you do do an&nbsp;
excellent job of, like, laying that out. Hmm. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:54.880
how do we. How do we use this to engage with,&nbsp;
like, these institutions that control us and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:03.600
create tautology that we can kind of break down&nbsp;
and see are just objectively false, but yet they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:10.160
stick to them and then even, you know, utilize&nbsp;
other legal means and like means of power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:16.640
to to keep them to keep kind of these these&nbsp;
things true. Like, I'll use that in quotes.&nbsp;

